To fix the CORS error in Next.js when using WordPress as a backend, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your Next.js project by running the following command:
```bash
npm install --save cors
```
2. In your `next.config.js` file, add the following configuration to enable CORS:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        headers: [
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', value: 'true' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin', value: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', value: 'GET,OPTIONS,PATCH,DELETE,POST' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', value: 'X-CSRF-Token, X-Requested-With, Accept, Accept-Version, Content-Length, Content-MD5, Content-Type, Date, X-Api-Version' }
        ]
      }
    ];
  }
};
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site.
3. In your WordPress site, make sure to set the `WP_REST_API_SERIALIZATION` constant to `json` in your `wp-config.php` file:
```php
define( 'WP_REST_API_SERIALIZATION', 'json' );
```
4. Finally, update your Next.js code to use the `fetch` API or a library like `axios` to make API requests to your WordPress site. Make sure to include the necessary CORS headers in your requests.

After completing these steps, you should no longer encounter the CORS error when making API requests from your Next.js project to your WordPress site.

