To achieve this, you will need to use a database trigger and a script (in your preferred programming language) to check for the break condition and display the reminder message.

Here's a high-level overview of the steps:

1. Create a database trigger on the `work_sessions` table that fires after each insert or update. This trigger will start a background process that checks for the break condition.
2. In the trigger function, retrieve the current work session details from the database.
3. Calculate the elapsed time since the start of the work session.
4. If the elapsed time is greater than 600 seconds (10 minutes), prompt the user to take a break.
5. Repeat step 3 periodically (e.g., every minute) to check for the break condition.

Here's an example of how you might implement this in MySQL using a stored procedure:

```sql
CREATE PROCEDURE check_for_break()
BEGIN
    DECLARE current_session_id INT;
    DECLARE elapsed_time FLOAT;

    SELECT id INTO current_session_id FROM work_sessions ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 1;

    IF current_session_id IS NULL THEN
        RETURN;
    END IF;

    SELECT TIMEDIFF(NOW(), start_time) INTO elapsed_time FROM work_sessions WHERE id = current_session_id;

    IF elapsed_time > 600 THEN
        -- Prompt the user to take a break
        SET @message = 'It has been 10 minutes since you started working. Take a break!';
        CALL send_reminder(@message);
    END IF;

    -- Schedule the next check
    CALL schedule_next_check();
END;

CALL check_for_break();
```

This stored procedure retrieves the current work session details, calculates the elapsed time, and prompts the user to take a break if the elapsed time is greater than 600 seconds. It also schedules the next check to run every minute using the `schedule_next_check` procedure.

