To make the generated planets less clustered together, you can modify your `generateRandomPlanets` function to ensure there's enough space between each planet. Here's an updated version of the function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  // Ensure there's enough space between each planet
  for (let i = 0; i < planets.length - 1; i++) {
    for (let j = i + 1; j < planets.length; j++) {
      const distance = planets[i].position.distanceTo(planets[j].position);
      if (distance < minDistance) {
        planets.splice(j, 1);
        i--;
      }
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

This modified function checks for collisions between all pairs of planets and removes any that are too close together. You can adjust the `minDistance` parameter to control the minimum distance between planets.

