To add custom CSS to a Shopify section and make the image smaller, you can use the `:before` pseudo-element to create an overlay that reduces the size of the image. Here's an example:

```css
.feature-row__image {
  position: relative;
}

.feature-row__image:before {
  content: "";
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
  z-index: 999;
}

.feature-row__image.small {
  width: 80%;
  height: 80%;
}
```

To apply this CSS to a specific section, you can add the `small` class to the section's `data-section` attribute in your Shopify theme's code. For example:

```html
<div data-section="feature-row" class="small">
  <!-- Your section content -->
</div>
```

This will make the image in the specified section smaller by 20%. You can adjust the value of the `width` and `height` properties to achieve the desired effect.

To identify if a given sentence in Go contains a double negative, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a slice of specific negative words.
2. Check if the sentence contains any of them.
3. Check if the sentence contains the phrase "not" followed by that negative word.
4. If both conditions are satisfied, print a message indicating that the sentence contains a double negative.

Here's the Go code to accomplish this task:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
)

func main() {
	negativeWords := []string{"not", "never", "no", "none"}
	sentence := "He isn't not worried about the coronavirus situation"

	for _, word := range negativeWords {
		if sentence.Contains(word) {
			if sentence.Contains("not") && sentence.Contains(word) {
				fmt.Println("The sentence contains a double negative.")
			}
		}
	}
}
```

In this code, we first create a slice of specific negative words. Then, we check if the sentence contains any of them using the `Contains` method. If the sentence contains a negative word, we check if the sentence also contains the phrase "not" followed by that negative word. If both conditions are satisfied, we print a message indicating that the sentence contains a double negative.

Note that this code is case-sensitive, so it will only detect double negatives if they are written in all lowercase or all uppercase letters. If you want to make it case-insensitive, you can use the `strings.ToLower` function to convert the sentence to lowercase before checking for negative words and phrases.

